Diagnostic: write a shared atlas out to disk at every handoff

Measurement-only, off unless `WINE_ALTARS_DUMP_ATLAS` is set in the
environment; then the first 24 snapshots go to `/tmp/altars-atlas-<tex>-NN.ppm`
(colour) and `.pgm` (the alpha plane on its own).

It exists because of a question the instruments in this tree could not answer.
Word's Backstage navigation rail comes up with no icons and no labels -- not
black (that was 0015), just the background colour -- and so do the "新建"
heading, the 最近/收藏夹/与我共享 tabs and the search box, while the template
card captions beside them render.  `+d2d` says Office **does** rasterise the
missing ones: every one of the six rail labels appears as a `DrawGlyphRun`
with its own text, on the same device context, in the same BeginDraw batch,
through the same call sequence as the captions that do appear --

    DrawTextLayout -> DrawGlyphRun L"\5f00\59cb"     (开始)
      -> CreateBitmap {24,11}  (the A8 coverage mask)
      -> CreateBitmapBrush -> CreateRectangleGeometry (130,3)-(154,14)
      -> FillGeometry

    DrawTextLayout -> DrawGlyphRun L"\7a7a\767d\6587\6863"   (空白文档)
      -> CreateBitmap {48,12}
      -> CreateBitmapBrush -> CreateRectangleGeometry (364,3)-(412,15)
      -> FillGeometry

so "was it drawn" is answered and the next question is "did it reach the
surface that gets composited".  Office composites its UI out of three
2048x1280 MISC_SHARED_KEYEDMUTEX atlases, and `shared_surface_readback` (0015)
already has the whole atlas in system memory on the CPU at every handoff --
one `fwrite` away from being readable.  Nothing else in this tree has that.

What it found, 24 snapshots across one start-up: the atlases carry the
elements that appear on screen -- 空白文档, 书法字帖, 最近, the template
thumbnails, the blue pills -- and in none of them is any of
开始/新建/打开/帐户/反馈/选项, "Word" or 更多模板 to be found.

**The alpha plane is why there are two files.** The first version wrote only
the colour channels, and these atlases are premultiplied BGRA: black text at
full coverage is (0,0,0,255), which is exactly what "nothing was ever drawn
here" looks like in a colour dump.  With the alpha plane the answer came back
the same -- the labels are absent from both -- but it was one `fwrite` between
a measurement and a guess.

**And that finding does not mean what it looks like.**  Recorded here because
the patch would otherwise read as evidence for a conclusion this tree has
since retracted (see "The blank rail" in docs/office365-under-wine.md).  The
atlas is a **recycling cache**: `patches/office/0017-*` shows the same string
filled at a different rectangle every time it is needed --

    开始    (130,3)-(154,14)   then   (1,3)-(25,14)
    空白文档  (364,3)-(412,15)   then  (1967,3)-(2015,15), (27,3)-(75,15), ...

-- so a snapshot at handoff N holds only whoever occupies those slots at that
moment, and absence from it is not evidence about anything.  D2D fill
rectangles do not map 1:1 onto atlas pixels either.  What the dumps are
genuinely good for is the opposite question: confirming that something *is*
there, and comparing two runs of the same frame.

PPM/PGM rather than PNG on purpose: no library, no allocation beyond the FILE
buffer, and nothing to go wrong in a path that runs inside a render loop.
--- a/dlls/d3d11/shared.c
+++ b/dlls/d3d11/shared.c
@@ -34,6 +34,8 @@
  * under llvmpipe, and under +d3d11 tracing, it never reproduced.
  */
 
+#include <stdio.h>
+
 #include "d3d11_private.h"
 
 WINE_DEFAULT_DEBUG_CHANNEL(d3d11);
@@ -170,6 +172,64 @@
     ID3D11DeviceContext_Unmap(context, (ID3D11Resource *)staging, 0);
     ID3D11DeviceContext_Release(context);
 
+    /* altars diagnostic: with WINE_ALTARS_DUMP_ATLAS set, write each snapshot
+     * out as a PPM.  Office composites its UI from these atlases, so "is the
+     * label in the atlas and not on the screen, or never in the atlas at all"
+     * is the question that splits a compositing bug from a rasterising one,
+     * and this is the only place the whole atlas is already in system memory
+     * on the CPU.  Off unless asked for: it writes ~8MB a frame. */
+    if (getenv("WINE_ALTARS_DUMP_ATLAS"))
+    {
+        static LONG dumped;
+        LONG n = InterlockedIncrement(&dumped);
+
+        if (n <= 24)
+        {
+            char path[256];
+            FILE *f;
+
+            sprintf(path, "/tmp/altars-atlas-%p-%02ld.ppm", texture, (long)n);
+            if ((f = fopen(path, "wb")))
+            {
+                unsigned int px;
+
+                fprintf(f, "P6\n%u %u\n255\n", texture->desc.Width, rows);
+                for (y = 0; y < rows; ++y)
+                {
+                    const unsigned char *line = (const unsigned char *)shared->bits
+                            + (size_t)y * shared->pitch;
+                    for (px = 0; px < texture->desc.Width; ++px)
+                        fwrite((const char[]){ line[px * 4 + 2], line[px * 4 + 1],
+                                line[px * 4] }, 1, 3, f);
+                }
+                fclose(f);
+                TRACE("altars-atlas dumped %s.\n", path);
+            }
+
+            /* And the alpha plane on its own.  These atlases are premultiplied
+             * BGRA: black text at full coverage is (0,0,0,255), which is
+             * indistinguishable from "nothing here" in the colour dump above
+             * and perfectly visible here.  Reading only the colour channels
+             * once produced a confident and wrong "the label never reaches the
+             * atlas". */
+            sprintf(path, "/tmp/altars-atlas-%p-%02ld.pgm", texture, (long)n);
+            if ((f = fopen(path, "wb")))
+            {
+                fprintf(f, "P5\n%u %u\n255\n", texture->desc.Width, rows);
+                for (y = 0; y < rows; ++y)
+                {
+                    const unsigned char *line = (const unsigned char *)shared->bits
+                            + (size_t)y * shared->pitch;
+                    unsigned int px;
+
+                    for (px = 0; px < texture->desc.Width; ++px)
+                        fputc(line[px * 4 + 3], f);
+                }
+                fclose(f);
+            }
+        }
+    }
+
     TRACE("Read %ux%u out of texture %p into shared state %p.\n",
             texture->desc.Width, rows, texture, shared);
     return S_OK;
